= Introduction

Now is the time to decide how the IBX should be built.
The MTA is slated to release the draft environmental impact statement (DEIS) for the IBX in #link("https://ibx.mta.info/#:~:text=anticipated%20release%20in-,late%202026,-)")[late 2026]#super[#link(<src2>)[\[2\]]].
The public response to that will be one of the last times to shape the broadest contours of the project design, as the final environmental impact statement (FEIS) will lock in major decisions.
What broad type of service should be run, and what type of construction is needed to enable that service, should be decided now.

Originally, a single street-running section with truly tight curves forced the design for the IBX to be light rail.
That section, by All Faiths Cemetery, will thankfully now be #link("https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HEfH7R6j6QY")[built as a tunnel]#super[#link(<src3>)[\[3\]]], as #link("https://www.etany.org/ibx-all-faiths-tunnel")[ETA had previously pushed for]#super[#link(<src4>)[\[4\]]], relieving a major constraint.
The IBX right-of-way is now guaranteed to be completely grade-separated, significantly improving speed, reliability, and achievable frequency.
One key way grade separation does this is by unlocking better service and rolling stock designs.
Since the project is no longer planning on street-running, many earlier constraints no longer apply, and given the growing needs of IBX ridership, there are better choices for the IBX going forward.
Now is the time for the MTA to seize the opportunity and commit to the right set of technologies.
To date, however, the agency has been sending mixed signals.

Since announcing the All Faiths tunnel, the MTA has started to describe the IBX more as a light metro rather than traditional American light rail.
Jamie Torres-Springer, head of MTA Construction & Development, described the IBX at that press conference as a #link("https://www.youtube.com/live/HEfH7R6j6QY?t=911s")[“light rail metro,”]#super[#link(<src5>)[\[5\]]] and at IBX Open Houses, MTA IBX staff have also said that the IBX will now be much more akin to a light metro.

However, other parts of the MTA perhaps didn’t get the memo.
The IBX website still #link("https://ibx.mta.info/about/#:~:text=to%20travel%20through-,tight%20curves,-Fit%20through%20the")[claims light rail is necessary to deal with tight curves]#super[#link(<src6>)[\[6\]]], even though, due to the removal of the street-running segment, the #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=992")[minimum IBX curve radius]#super[#link(<src7>)[\[7\]]] is exceptionally gentle.
Indeed, it is #link("https://www.wmata.com/business/procurement/solicitations/documents/General%20Design%20Criteria%20-%208K%202017%2001%2024.pdf#page=4")[twice as gentle as that of the Washington Metro]#super[#link(<src8>)[\[8\]]], which is more than enough to permit any type of train to run at over 30 mph#footnote[sqrt((6 in cant + 3 in cant deficiency) \* 465 ft curve radius \* 9.81 m/s#super[2] / (1435 mm rail gauge + 2.71875 in rail width) = 32.5 mph] on this curve (see #link("#rolling-stock")[Rolling Stock] for more detail).
Failure to cull unneeded constraints like this from the rolling stock procurement process will likely drive up costs without benefiting IBX riders in any way.

Therefore, while there have been some welcome and encouraging words, the MTA has yet to commit to the concrete details underlying them: high-floor light metro vehicles.
And more importantly, they are missing one crucial piece: automation.

There is one type of light rail that is clearly ideal for the IBX: #emph[automated light metro] (ALM).
The key properties of ALMs are:

- Automated operations
- Short trains
- High frequency

ALM technology would enable the IBX to feature significantly higher all-day frequencies from day one by lowering operating costs.
ALM allows headways (the time between trains) as low as every 90 seconds, as seen in many existing systems around the world today.
Initial ridership may not warrant 90 second headways, but ALM would allow peak and off-peak headways much lower than the MTA currently plans, increasing ridership from day one and future-proofing the IBX for significant growth.
ALM would also mean installing platform screen doors (PSDs) to protect riders from the risks of falling onto the tracks, the #link("https://communityofmetros.org/research-enhancing-platform-safety-without-platform-doors/")[biggest source of risk to riders]#super[#link(<src9>)[\[9\]]] on the subway today.

= Rising Ridership

IBX ridership projections have steadily risen as plans materialize and improve.
The design of the IBX must be future-proofed to accommodate this rising ridership, and utilizing ALM technology is critical to do so in a cost-effective manner.

In particular, ridership projections have risen 82%,#footnote[(160,000/day) / (87,800/day) = 1.822] from #link("https://www.mta.info/document/72081#page=16")[87,800/day]#super[#link(<src10>)[\[10\]]] in the #link("https://www.mta.info/document/72081")[initial feasibility study in 2022]#super[#link(<src11>)[\[11\]]], to #link("https://www.mta.info/document/103691#page=5")[115,000/day]#super[#link(<src12>)[\[12\]]] in the #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891")[planning and environmental linkages (PEL) study]#super[#link(<src13>)[\[13\]]], and to #link("https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/governor-hochul-announces-interborough-express-advancing-planning-active-phase#:~:text=ridership%20projections%20to-,160%2C000,-per%20day%2C%20up")[160,000/day]#super[#link(<src14>)[\[14\]]] in the more detailed and higher-speed 2025 design.
This is higher than the ridership of the #link("https://anita.garden/nycriders/")[G train (149,000/day)]#super[#link(<src15>)[\[15\]]] and the vehicle count on the #link("https://www.nyc.gov/html/dot/html/infrastructure/bqecentral.shtml#:~:text=130%2C000%20vehicles%20use%20the%20BQE%20daily")[BQE (130,000/day)]#super[#link(<src16>)[\[16\]]], both of which also connect Brooklyn and Queens.
This has been driven by all of the following factors:

- Refinement of the ridership model as plans for the project have solidified
- Improved station siting
- A #link("https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/video-audio-photos-rush-transcript-governor-hochul-announces-interborough-express-advancing")[31% ]#super[#link(<src17>)[\[17\]]]increase in speed#footnote[#link("https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/video-audio-photos-rush-transcript-governor-hochul-announces-interborough-express-advancing#:~:text=10%20minutes%20less%20than%20the%20initial%20estimate")[42 min]#super[#link(<src70>)[\[70\]]] / #link("https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/video-audio-photos-rush-transcript-governor-hochul-announces-interborough-express-advancing#:~:text=32%20minutes")[32 min]#super[#link(<src71>)[\[71\]]] = 1.3125. The original LRT runtime was #link("https://www.mta.info/document/103691#page=5")[39 min in the PEL]#super[#link(<src12>)[\[12\]]], but IBX staff told ETA street-running delays were initially underestimated, hence the 10 minutes faster quoted by Hochul.] after #link("https://www.etany.org/ibx-all-faiths-tunnel")[ETA’s analysis]#super[#link(<src4>)[\[4\]]] and #link("https://www.amny.com/nyc-transit/queens-graveyard-hochul-interborough-express/")[Ben Brachfeld’s reporting]#super[#link(<src18>)[\[18\]]] successfully pressured the MTA to #link("https://www.6sqft.com/mta-awards-166m-design-contract-for-interborough-express/")[drop street running]#super[#link(<src19>)[\[19\]]] around All Faiths Cemetery

