  #link("https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harsimus_Cove_HBLR_4th_St_RR_Crossing.jpg")[Credit: Sebastian Sinisterra]#super[#link(<src57>)[\[57\]]]],
)

Finally, in contrast to ALMs or heavy rail systems like the subway, light rail systems in the US often force passengers to cross the tracks to get to the opposite-side platform, even when they run in private rights-of-way and not on the street.
For example, Boston’s Green Line Extension designed Ball Square and East Somerville to require crossing the live tracks at-grade, as a last-minute cost-saving measure.
Similarly, Jersey City’s Hudson-Bergen Light Rail (HBLR) requires passengers to cross the tracks at many stations such as Newport, Harsimus Cove, and Jersey Avenue.
This is not acceptable for an ALM system, which requires higher levels of safety as a driver cannot operate the train by sight.
Even without automation, pedestrian grade crossings at stations force trains to enter those stations at reduced speed, driving up runtimes.
The MTA’s 32 minute runtime target, for example, would be difficult to meet if grade crossings for passengers were built.

= Rolling Stock

Almost four years after first selecting the IBX’s mode as light rail,#footnote[The MTA chose light rail transit (LRT) as the mode for the IBX on January 10, 2023 in the #link("https://www.mta.info/document/103691")[PEL study]#super[#link(<src92>)[\[92\]]].] the MTA has still yet to officially make a rolling stock procurement decision.
As they have reiterated many times, they have been designing everything to be maximally flexible so that no choice is prematurely locked in.
This can potentially be valuable for projects very early in the design phase, but it comes at a cost as the project evolves: the design has to constantly consider a multitude of alternatives, and cannot optimize around the best alternative.
At this point in the project, it is time for the IBX to settle on a technology, and abandon the no-longer relevant options considered earlier.

Prior to the complete grade separation of the corridor, trains would have needed to run on city streets, and would thus have necessitated very sharp curves.
These factors might have justified light rail vehicles (LRVs) designed for this purpose.
The All Faiths Tunnel removes the need for street running.
However, the MTA is still claiming that LRVs are required in order to “#link("https://ibx.mta.info/about/")[travel through tight curves]#super[#link(<src58>)[\[58\]]].”
Yet the tightest curve on the IBX route has a #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=992")[radius of 465 ft]#super[#link(<src7>)[\[7\]]]#footnote[The #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=992")[IBX1 LRT-18]#super[#link(<src7>)[\[7\]]] curve in the PEL study has a radius of 465 ft. The wider-radius (917 ft) #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=992")[IBX2 LRT-11]#super[#link(<src7>)[\[7\]]] curve is for the Brooklyn Army Terminal station option further west, but the MTA has decided on the eastern option closer to 2 Av, which has the 465 ft curve radius.
The next tightest curve is #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=994")[IBX1 LRT-36]#super[#link(<src93>)[\[93\]]] and #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=994")[IBX2 LRT-37]#super[#link(<src93>)[\[93\]]] at 917 ft.], so generous that any train can fit.
The tightest curve on the NYC subway has a radius of #link("https://www.mta.info/document/172206#page=6")[100±10 ft]#super[#link(<src59>)[\[59\]]]#footnote[We’re not sure where the ~100 ft curve is, but the #link("https://www.nycsubway.org/wiki/Chapter_03._General_Arrangement_for_Construction#:~:text=150-,South%20Ferry%20loop,-111")[old South Ferry loop]#super[#link(<src60>)[\[60\]]] is close (111 ft) and is still in occasional revenue service.
Other tighter curves may be in yard trackage.], with the #link("https://www.nycsubway.org/wiki/Chapter_03._General_Arrangement_for_Construction#:~:text=150-,South%20Ferry%20loop,-111")[old South Ferry loop being 111 ft]#super[#link(<src60>)[\[60\]]].
Even the Metro in Washington, DC, designed to avoid the squeal of the tight curves of the oldest sections of the NYC Subway, has a #link("https://www.wmata.com/business/procurement/solicitations/documents/General%20Design%20Criteria%20-%208K%202017%2001%2024.pdf#page=4")[minimum curve radius of 225 ft]#super[#link(<src8>)[\[8\]]], less than half of that of the IBX.
The only tight curves were the #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=1014")[100 ft street-running curves]#super[#link(<src61>)[\[61\]]]#footnote[In the former street-running section, there were multiple 100 ft curves: #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=1014")[IBX1 LRT-647]#super[#link(<src61>)[\[61\]]], #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=1014")[IBX1 LRT-661]#super[#link(<src61>)[\[61\]]], #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=1014")[IBX2 LRT641]#super[#link(<src61>)[\[61\]]], #link("https://www.mta.info/document/114891#page=1014")[IBX2 LRT656]#super[#link(<src61>)[\[61\]]].] that have since been removed with the All Faiths tunnel.

